## Context

Child of 0700. Author decision of 2026-10-01: a close-reading core of 200–400
works is too heavy. The binding constraint is REL's envelope (about 12,500
words including references; outline target about 2,500 words of references,
so roughly 75–100 cited works). History-of-economic-thought journals cite
40–100 works; JEL surveys 150–300. The full corpus is cited once, as the
deposited dataset with its DOI.

## Decision: three tiers

| Tier | Works | Depth | Who |
|---|---|---|---|
| Deep read | 60–100 | full text, positioned in the narrative; most cited | author |
| Skim | 100–150 | abstract, introduction, conclusion, data section if relevant; validates the coding, picks exemplars | author, with agent-prepared notes |
| Coded only | the rest of the crisp REL set (about 6–13k, derived estimate) | L1 structured coding of title + abstract; L2 full-text coding only where available | agents |

Deep-read selection, each item earning its place:
- 2–4 exemplars per stream (about 8–10 streams from the bibliometric map);
- the classics of each period (1990–2006, 2007–2014, 2015–2025);
- the key papers on the four accounting controversies (loan valuation, marker
  credibility, private-finance attribution, additionality);
- a few Southern and non-English works, so the core is not only the citation elite.

## Middle layer (to be decomposed into child tickets after the harvest closes)
- **L1** abstract-level coding of every included work: causal arc
  (`conception/carte-causale-icf-co2.md`), method, data source named, region,
  instrument, controversy and position. Validated as the screen was (reference
  set, recall, test–retest).
- **L2** full-text coding restricted to the deep-read and skim tiers, plus the
  data-source question on the open-access share; coverage reported per stratum.
- **Full-text access**: OpenAlex/Unpaywall, RePEc, CORE, ISTEX/bibCNRS; the
  paywalled remainder is acquired only for the deep-read tier (Zotero, read
  before cite). TDM under EU DSM Directive art. 3.

## Reuse beyond REL (author request, 2026-10-01)

The reading and coding serve the author's book and the history-of-economic-thought
paper as well as REL, so nothing is built REL-only:
- **Reading notes** for the deep-read and skim tiers live in Zotero (read before
  cite), in one structured format, not in REL manuscript files. Each note
  records who wrote it, when, and from which institution; the argument and its
  lineage (whom it answers or builds on); the instruments and data it relies on;
  and the period, using the three acts 1990–2006, 2007–2014, 2015–2025.
- **The L1/L2 coding** is a versioned dataset in the repo, keyed by pool work id.
  Each deliverable draws its own subset and does not recode. The schema includes
  the history-relevant fields (actors and institutions, period, intellectual
  lineage, instrument circulation) besides the REL fields.
- **Deep-read selection** carries a tag per item naming which deliverables need
  it (REL / HET paper / book). The core is shared where the needs overlap, and an
  item needed only by the book or the HET paper is marked so REL's word budget
  does not drive it out.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Deep-read list (60–100) chosen by the rules above, with the reason per item
- [ ] Skim list (100–150) with agent-prepared notes
- [ ] L1 coding of the crisp REL set, validated
- [ ] L2 coding of the deep-read and skim tiers, coverage per stratum reported
- [ ] Full text in hand for every deep-read item
- [ ] Reading notes in Zotero in the shared format; coding dataset reusable by the HET paper and the book
